WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.090
- [Instructor] We've spent
several videos already

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:05.060
talking about graphs like you see here.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.810
This is the graph for a particular firm.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.360
Maybe it's making donuts

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.690
so it's in the donut industry.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.630
And we can see how the marginal cost

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.150
relates to the average variable cost

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:13.983
and average total cost.

00:00:13.983 --> 00:00:16.300
We go into some depth several videos ago

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.800
but we see that trend that marginal cost

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.350
can trend down initially

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.250
because as quantity increases

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.980
each incremental unit could benefit

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.840
from things like specialization.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.600
And then the marginal cost,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.460
the cost of each incremental unit

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.560
as a function of quantity, could go up

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.870
because of things like coordination costs.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.040
And then we've also seen

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.860
how that relates to average variable cost,

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:37.990
that while marginal cost

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.940
is below average variable cost,

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.440
every incremental unit is going

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.580
to bring down the average variable cost.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.750
But then when marginal cost

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.750
crosses average variable cost,

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.240
well, now every incremental unit

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.320
is going to bring up the
average variable cost.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.540
And the same thing happens

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.700
once it crosses the average total cost.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:54.740
And of course the difference

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.290
between, for any give quantity

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.930
between the average total cost

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.440
and the average variable cost,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.480
that is the average fixed cost.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.680
Now, with that out of the way

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.630
we're going to think about how this firm

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.640
would react under different
market conditions.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.700
We're going to assume that it's

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.730
in a very competitive, or we could say

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.360
a perfectly competitive market,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.120
and so it is a price taker.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.030
And so let's first imagine

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.770
what would be a positive scenario

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.520
for this firm?

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.000
Let's imagine the price up here.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.800
So let's call this P sub-one

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.760
and in the previous video we already said

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.680
it would be rational for
a profit-maximizing firm

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.610
to produce at a quantity

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.880
where the marginal cost

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.900
and the marginal revenue meet.

00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:42.050
If we're talking about
a competitive market,

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.670
then this price right over here

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.060
is not going to be a function

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.330
of the firm's quantity,

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:48.540
so that's why it's horizontal,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.130
and it would be the same thing

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.750
as the marginal revenue.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.420
So, in this situation, at P sub-one,

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:00.050
the firm would produce Q sub-one,

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.520
and this is a good situation for the firm,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.960
because the price that it's getting

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.000
is higher than its average total cost.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.230
And so there is going to be

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.610
a nice amount of profit for this firm.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.010
The profit is going to be the price

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.300
minus the average total
cost at that quantity

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.930
times the actual quantity.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.940
So, because P one is greater

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.880
than the average total cost,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.900
we have a situation where
the firm is profitable.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.480
Firm is profitable.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.720
It would want to stay in the market.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.680
But because you have a profitable firm

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.070
in this market and you're likely to have

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.010
many profitable firms in that market,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.350
it will probably attract entrance.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.303
Attract,

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.300
attract entrance.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.450
Other people might say,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.090
hey, I want to make just as much money

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.790
as this donut company right over here,

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.530
then this firm, and so you'll probably

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.430
have more and more
entrance into the market,

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.430
which will probably reduce the prices.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.550
Now, they could reduce the prices

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.100
until you get to a price that looks

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.660
something like this.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.550
So I will call that P sub-two.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.420
Now, a profit-maximizing firm

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.740
in this world would keep producing

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.600
until the marginal cost is equal

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.440
to the marginal revenue,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.920
which in this case is the price.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.600
And so this would be,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.240
my lines aren't completely straight there,

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.430
but you get the idea.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.993
So that's Q sub-two.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.560
Now, in this situation, P sub-two

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.690
is equal to the average total cost

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.770
so the firm is breakeven.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.270
It's not running at a loss or a profit.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.510
So it is breakeven.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.110
And so here the firm is neutral

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.770
about whether in the long run

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:43.440
it stays in the market
or it exits the market.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.370
But you're no longer
likely attracting entrance.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:48.280
So no longer

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.850
attracting,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.703
attracting entrance.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.790
But it does make sense for the firm

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.070
to keep operating at this situation

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:01.090
even in the long run,

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.790
because it is at least breakeven.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.350
Now let's imagine another scenario.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.050
Let's imagine this price level.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.950
So for whatever reason the market price

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.120
gets to that as we've talked about.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.560
A rational firm would be producing

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.720
at Q sub-three.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.470
And at P sub-three right over here

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:21.710
there's some interesting things.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:24.310
Because P sub-three is less

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:26.610
than your average total cost,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.960
your firm is running at a loss.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.690
It's running at a loss here.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.833
So running, so firm,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.403
firm not profitable.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.090
Not profitable.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:39.923
Now, you might say,

00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:41.810
well, what is this firm likely to do?

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.200
Would it just shut down?

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.380
Well, in the short run it would not

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.010
make sense for this firm to shut down,

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:50.320
because the price that it's getting

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.680
is still higher than its
average variable cost.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.730
In the short run, the fixed cost,

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:56.260
they've already been spent.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.340
So, you might as well get as much

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.820
incremental profit on
the margin as you can.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.230
And so as long as the price is higher

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.460
than the average variable cost,

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.530
well, outside of their fixed cost

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.220
they're still making some money

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.000
to make up those fixed costs.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.950
So you have two things going on.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.110
So, they would stay operating

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:21.430
in the short run, stay operating,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.660
operating in the short run,

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.650
short run.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.500
But what would this
firm do in the long run?

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.210
Well, in the long run it makes no sense

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.460
to have a, to be in a market

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.040
where you can't make a profit.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.020
So in the long run it will exit.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.390
So, it will exit

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:42.497
in the long run.

00:05:42.497 --> 00:05:44.350
And in general, the terminology

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:45.957
when people are talking about "well,

00:05:45.957 --> 00:05:47.730
"do you start or stop in the short run,"

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.377
they usually talk about "do you either

00:05:49.377 --> 00:05:51.550
"shut down or operate in the short run?"

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.510
And in the long run we say hey,

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.380
are you going to sell your factories,

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.210
or somehow dismantle them,

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.570
or are you gonna build new factories?

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:00.840
That's all about exiting

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.790
or entering the industry.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.500
And of course, you have another

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.660
even worse scenario for this firm,

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:09.130
which might be down here,

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.310
where you have price sub-four.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.780
Here, in theory, this is where

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.990
we intersect the marginal cost curve,

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:17.740
Q sub-four.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.220
Now here it makes no sense

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.860
for the company to operate at all.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.890
So, because P sub-four is less

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.830
than the average total cost,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:29.010
you would want to exit in the long run,

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:32.168
exit in long run, exit the market.

00:06:32.168 --> 00:06:33.810
Well, you wouldn't even
wait for that long,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.340
wait to sell your factories.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.060
Because P sub-four is less

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.960
than your average variable cost,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.070
you would also just shut down,

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:42.173
shut down,

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.780
in the short run.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:48.860
So, a big picture.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:50.170
From a firm's point of view,

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.100
you obviously want to be at P one

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.470
where you make a profit,

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.800
but you might attract entrance.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.840
At P sub-two, you as a firm

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.300
in the long run are neutral

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.040
versus exiting the market

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:02.130
or entering the market,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.780
or other people entering the market.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.380
You're at breakeven.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:09.420
At P sub-three, in the long run,

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.260
you'd want to exit because
you're not profitable,

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.410
if the prices stay at P sub-three.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:17.080
Your price is below
your average total cost

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.210
at the rational quantity to produce,

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:22.160
so in the long run you would exit.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.550
But because P sub-three is greater

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:27.390
than your average variable cost

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.310
at the rational quantity,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:32.120
you would stay operating in the short run.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.290
And then, the last scenario, of course,

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.160
is P sub-four where the price gets so low

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.120
that just doesn't make sense

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.163
to even operate another moment.

